[; Fetch a vendor CSV, parse it into typed records, and count the rows that
   satisfy a schema — the minimum viable agentic data-ingest pipeline, written
   as three [?def]s so each stage is separately nameable and testable.

   Rewritten for the v0.8.0 surface (RULED: VC-28, completing the corpus-wide
   migration the other five programs got on 2026-08-25). The original was
   pre-reshape throughout: `:scope`/`:returns`/`:pure` colon-slot modifiers on
   [?def] (retired — grammar [152a] refuses them by name), the
   `[?http-client :method … :yield-body $b :in …]` request form (the directive
   is a client HANDLE constructor now; the one-shot verbs live in
   cx-stdlib/http), `[?let $x = … :in]`, `[?for … :where … :yield …]`, and
   `[field :name "email"]` colon-attributes.

   Two things the rewrite MEASURED rather than assumed:

   1. `[$csv:parse]` yields all-string cells, so a `type="int"` field fails
      with `score: expected int, got string`. `[$csv:parse-with-schema]` with
      `{score: :int}` is the surface's answer — column coercion at parse time,
      which is why the schema names the column twice (once to coerce, once to
      bound-check). This was hypothesis 2 in the write-up's gap log; it is now
      a measured answer, not a hypothesis.
   2. A CXDM map (the csv row shape) IS accepted by `[$validate:validate-shape]`
      — the `$value::any` signature is honoured. Hypothesis 3, closed.

   The program still exits non-zero under the corpus gate, and that is the
   HONEST outcome: the fetch needs the `net` capability, which the offline gate
   does not grant, so it refuses with CXER0271 before any byte moves. Run it
   for real with:
     cx --allow-net=crm.example.com corpus/rosetta/21-fetch-csv-validate.cx ]

[?lib 'cx-stdlib/http']
[?lib 'cx-stdlib/csv']
[?lib 'cx-stdlib/validate']

[?const LEAD_SCHEMA [schema
  [field name="email"      type="string" pattern=".+@.+\..+"]
  [field name="first_name" type="string" min-length=1]
  [field name="last_name"  type="string" min-length=1]
  [field name="score"      type="int"    min=0 max=100]]]

[; Stage 1 — fetch + parse. `score` is coerced at parse time so the schema's
   int bound has an int to bound. ]
[?def fetch-leads scope=public impure [effects [net]] [returns [sequence any]] ($csv-url::string)
  [$csv:parse-with-schema [$http:body-text [$http:get $csv-url]] {score: :int}]]

[; Stage 2 — validate one row against the schema; returns [ok …] / [invalid …],
   the inspectable outcome, never the propagating [err] sentinel. ]
[?def validate-lead scope=public pure [returns element] ($row::any)
  [$validate:validate-shape $row LEAD_SCHEMA]]

[; Stage 3 — keep only the rows that validate, and count them. ]
[?def count-valid-leads scope=public impure [effects [net]] [returns int] ($csv-url::string)
  [$count [?for [in $row [$fetch-leads $csv-url]]
            [where [$validate:is-ok [$validate-lead $row]]]
            [yield $row]]]]

[$count-valid-leads "https://crm.example.com/exports/leads.csv"]
